A tinySA, as a second opinion
=============================

tinysa.py reads a tinySA over its USB serial line.  It is here for one
reason: os/inferno/radio/cc1120.c and the qemu model it was developed
against were written by the same hand, and a receiver reporting its
own signal strength is a board agreeing with itself.  A tinySA is an
instrument somebody else built, with its own front end, its own
calibration and its own reasons to be wrong.

	tools/tinysa/tinysa.py sweep 868000000 928000000 >tsa.txt
	tools/tinysa/tinysa.py cmd version rbw attenuate

and on the board, with the same band:

	; echo scan >/dev/radio0/ctl

Both are frequency-and-level, so the comparison is awk(1).  The two do
not measure the same thing and are not expected to agree in absolute
terms: a tinySA ULTRA at 100kHz resolution bandwidth and no input
attenuation is a calibrated receiver, while the CC1120's RSSI is its
own AGC's estimate with the manufacturer's reference offset applied
and no calibration of this board at all (see the RSSI offset note in
os/inferno/radio/cc1120.c).  What is worth comparing is where the
energy is, not how much of it there is.

What was measured, 22 September 2026
------------------------------------

The tinySA was on the bus for about a minute before it dropped off it,
which is long enough for its settings, its frequency axis and one
sweep, and not long enough for the repeat that would have made it
evidence.  What it gave:

	tinySA4, firmware tinySA4_v1.4-141-gf753dbf, HW V0.4.5.1.1
	rbw 100kHz, attenuate 0, sweeptime 1.153s
	450 points from 868.000 to 928.000MHz

The first twenty of those 450 points:

	868.000  -97.6      869.337  -85.0
	868.134  -74.1      869.470  -99.0
	868.267  -75.6      869.604  -90.5
	868.401  -71.5      869.738  -82.0
	868.535  -71.5      869.871  -80.5
	868.668  -72.5      870.005  -98.0
	868.802  -77.5      870.139  -82.5
	868.936  -89.5      870.272  -96.5
	869.069  -90.5      870.406  -85.5
	869.203  -94.5      870.539  -81.0

A plateau from 868.13 to 868.80MHz at -71 to -77dBm, fifteen to
twenty-five decibels above the bins on either side of it.  That is the
European SRD band, 868.0 to 868.6MHz, with something in it.

The board's own sweeps, taken minutes earlier, read their highest
value of all 61 steps at 868.000MHz in both runs: -101dBm in the
first, -105 in the second, against a median of -110 and -111.  Two
receivers, one of them not ours, putting the loudest thing in the band
in the same place.

That is corroboration and it is not a measurement.  One tinySA sweep
is one sweep; the tree's own rule is that a single sample is not
evidence, and it applies to the reference instrument as much as to the
board.  The comparison to make when the tinySA is on the bus again is
several sweeps of each, at the same time, with the same band.
